  "assumptions": [
    "Idealised small-swing pendulum: period T = 2π√(L/g) with g = 9.81 m/s², exact in this model and only approximately true of a real pendulum.",
    "The string is light and doesn't stretch, and the bob is a point mass at the end of the string. The bob is drawn as a small ball.",
    "There is no air resistance or friction, so the swing never dies down. The amplitude stays fixed at 10°.",
    "The motion has no stored state. Changing the length recomputes the swing from the current time at the new period, so the bob may jump to a new position.",
    "The 30 s timeline loops, so the swing may jump a little where the loop wraps around."
  ],
  "program": {
    "source": "const G=9.81,A=10*Math.PI/180;\nexport function evaluate({inputs,time}){const L=inputs.length;const T=2*Math.PI*Math.sqrt(L/G);const th=A*Math.cos(2*Math.PI*time/T);return{nodes:{arm:{quaternion:[0,0,Math.sin(th/2),Math.cos(th/2)]},strN:{position:[0,-L/2,0],scale:[1,L,1]},bobN:{position:[0,-L,0]}},readouts:{period:T,len:L,angle:th*180/Math.PI}};}"
  }
